Evan copied both clips. The progress bar appeared and vanished.

“Open the copies only,” Diane said.

He selected file 143\.

The waveform showed four seconds of irregular peaks. Evan expanded it until each second occupied several inches of screen. A low cluster sat near the beginning. A taller form rose around the phrase’s first syllable. The final second held a smaller pattern under the hiss.

He pressed play through the laptop speakers.

“Come here. I have a joke.”

The larger speaker gave the suspected words more body. To Diane, Wayne’s cadence emerged with greater confidence, though the sound also gained artifacts: a brittle edge, a pulsing undertone, a breath that might have belonged to the recorder’s automatic gain.

Ron looked at Diane.

She kept her attention on the waveform.

Evan replayed the file.

“Once,” she said.

“We need a monitoring level.”

“You have it.”

He stopped.

“File 144,” Abby said.

Evan opened the second clip.

The waveform appeared almost flat. Three narrow peaks occupied the center.

He pressed play.

Fabric moved close to the microphone. A fingertip struck the recorder casing. Under both sounds came a faint breathlike rush.

“That was in your pocket,” Diane said.

“My hand stayed off the record control.”

“The side button may have pressed against the fabric.”

“It requires a slide and hold.”

“You carried batteries, cables, and a badge against it.”

Evan enlarged the waveform. “There’s a voice under the contact noise.”

Ron shifted near the fire door. His jacket brushed the painted block wall.

“No labels,” Diane said.

“I haven’t labeled it.”

“You’re preparing everyone to hear one.”

He put on the headphones.

Abby kept the camera on him. “Say only what you can describe.”

Evan listened twice. On the third pass, Diane reached over and lifted one ear cup away from his head.

“One review,” she said.

He removed the headphones. “There are two syllables.”

“Frequency range?”

“I haven’t analyzed it.”

“Then you have a suspicion.”

Ron’s radio issued a brief tone.

Everyone turned.

The display showed no incoming call. Ron lifted it from his belt and pressed the side button once.

“Battery warning,” he said.

A second tone sounded through Diane’s radio a fraction later.

“Mine too.”

Evan’s attention moved from the radios to the laptop. “Record that.”

Abby tapped the camera body. “Already recording.”

“Separate audio.”

Diane folded the laptop halfway shut. “The copy is complete. We leave.”

Evan caught the screen before it closed. “We have four people here. Two independent audio sources. Known positions. This is better than anything upstairs.”

“This is a basement with uncontrolled routes, active radios, mechanical noise, and a group primed by a personal recording.”

“Then we account for those variables.”

“We remove ourselves from them.”

Ron stepped away from the fire door. “She said we’re done.”

Evan turned toward him. “You heard breathing in the west aisle. Give us sixty seconds there with the camera on everyone.”

Ron’s face hardened. “My account was an interview, not an invitation.”

“One minute could show the duct source.”

“Then maintenance can test it tomorrow.”

“During an occupied day, with the fans running?”

Ron looked toward Diane. Evan had found the weakness in every later test: altered conditions. The building’s routine could be reproduced only inside the routine itself. Tomorrow would bring different doors, temperatures, workers, practices, and sounds.

Diane opened the laptop again.

“Sixty seconds,” she said. “Nobody separates beyond sight. No names. No direct address. Abby keeps the wide frame. After sixty seconds, we leave regardless of result.”

Ron’s disappointment struck harder than anger.

“You’re giving him another test.”

“I’m closing the last argument.”

“That’s what every next test becomes.”

Diane had no answer that preserved her authority.

Evan moved quickly. He placed the laptop on a rolling whiteboard cart and opened a simple audio program. The camera feed remained untouched. He selected the empty track beneath the copied files.

“No editing,” Diane said.

“Live markers only.”

He named the track TEST\_1542.

Diane checked the clock. “Three forty-two. Positions.”

She placed Ron at the entrance to the old west aisle, ten feet beyond the junction and fully visible under the next fluorescent fixture. Abby remained beside the tripod. Evan stood at the laptop. Diane occupied the center, where she could reach all three routes.

“Radios off,” she said.

Ron switched his radio down. Diane switched hers down and set it on the shelf beside the electrical panel. Evan showed his empty hands to the camera. Abby panned across the floor, the fire door, bay C, the west aisle, and each participant before returning to the wide frame.

“Begin,” Diane said.

Evan pressed record.

The laptop’s counter advanced.

For eleven seconds, the recording captured only familiar basement sounds: ballast buzz, pipe contraction, a faint motor through the boiler-room wall.

At twelve seconds, the fire door moved inward by a fraction of an inch.

Its closer caught, held, and eased it back against the frame.

“Pressure equalization,” Diane said.

Evan marked the track.

At nineteen seconds, a low vibration crossed the return grille. The sound resembled a throat clearing under several layers of wall.

“Kitchen cart or exterior engine,” Diane said.

Another marker appeared.

At twenty-seven seconds, Ron raised one hand.

Diane faced him. “Describe.”

“Air movement.”

“Location?”

“Behind my right shoulder.”

The west aisle behind him stood empty for fifteen feet before turning toward the old mechanical office. A vertical duct occupied the near wall. Its seam carried a faint tremor.

“Step forward,” Diane said.

Ron moved one pace toward the junction.

The breathlike sound followed.

It came through the camera microphone as a broad rush with a soft pulse at its center.

Evan marked the track again.

Ron held his breath.

The rush continued for one second.

Then it stopped.

His face tightened.

“Test ends,” he said.

The laptop counter showed forty-one seconds.

“We have nineteen seconds left,” Evan said.

“Test ends.”

Evan kept recording.

Diane crossed to the laptop and pressed the space bar. The counter froze.

Ron walked toward them. His shoulders stayed level, yet his hands had closed into fists.

“I asked for this to stop upstairs,” he said. “I’m asking again. Everybody leaves.”

Abby reached for the tripod release.

Evan stood in front of the laptop. “We captured the same condition from your account.”

“You captured air in a duct.”

“You held your breath.”

“And I want out.”

“That reaction matters.”

Ron’s voice dropped. “My reaction belongs to me.”

The sentence stated the conflict plainly. Diane watched Evan absorb it as rebuke and usable audio.

She unplugged the recorder cable from the laptop.

“Pack up.”

Evan’s hand closed around the computer. “Let me save the test.”

“Save the raw track. No processing.”

He pressed the save command. A dialog appeared with the track name, sample rate, and location. The software assigned a project file and a separate cache folder.

Diane pointed. “Show the camera both paths.”

Abby moved closer.

Evan saved the project.

A faint sound passed through the laptop speakers.

Two syllables, low and soft.

Ron turned toward the west aisle.

“What was that?” Abby asked.

“Monitoring feedback,” Evan said.

“You stopped recording.”

“The input can remain live.”

Diane checked the audio meter. The bars sat at zero.

Evan opened the recorded track and moved to the last five seconds. “It’s on the file.”

“Play from thirty-five seconds,” Diane said.

He selected the range.

The speakers reproduced Ron’s breathing account, the rush behind him, and Diane’s approach to the laptop.

Then, beneath the click of the space bar, a low sequence broke into three uncertain pulses. Evan supplied words before anyone else spoke.

“Behind you now.”

Abby looked at Ron.

Ron backed away from the west aisle.

Diane heard a burst of air, the squeal of a cart wheel from somewhere above, and Evan’s own word you from an earlier question bleeding through the room. The final sound might have been now. It might have been the laptop fan changing speed.

Evan played it again and supplied the phrase before anyone else spoke.

“Behind you now.”

With each repetition, the same structure seemed clearer to the listeners.

“Stop,” Diane said.

He stopped.

“You placed that interpretation before the second pass.”

“I repeated what the file contains.”

“You repeated what you heard.”

Abby said, “I hear it too.”

Ron stared at the laptop. “So do I.”

Diane looked from one face to the next. Evan had supplied the phrase, and the others had repeated it until his interpretation resembled shared observation.

“Play the full minute once,” she said. “No commentary.”

Evan returned to the beginning.

The known sounds came in order. Door movement. Low vibration. Ron’s report. The rush. Diane ending the test. The click.

At the end, the voice seemed weaker without preparation.

Behind—

A scrape.

—now.

The middle word dissolved.

Ron heard enough. “We’re leaving.”

He picked up Abby’s tripod with one hand and turned toward the stairs.

Evan caught the laptop before the cart moved. “One more thing.”

“No,” Diane said.

“I marked each event while it happened. The markers prove the order.”

“Markers record your attention, not the event’s identity.”

“They preserve timing.”

“Then preserve them and close the computer.”

Evan opened the marker list.

The entries read DOOR, LOW VOICE, BREATH, ANSWER.

Diane pointed to the final label. “Change ANSWER to UNIDENTIFIED SOUND.”

“It answers the location.”

“No question appears before it.”

Evan changed the label. Ron’s interview file remained open in a second window beneath the test project. He had already imagined an exchange before creating one.

Diane removed the recorder from her pocket and held it out to Abby rather than Evan.

“Keep this visible. We leave together.”

Abby accepted it.

Evan saved the project.

A warning appeared: MISSING SOURCE TIMECODE. USE PROJECT POSITION?

Diane leaned closer. “Which source?”

Evan clicked cancel.

The project window returned. One small audio block at the end of the test track carried a generic name: RECOVERED\_07.

“That clip wasn’t in the raw track,” Diane said.

“It’s the final segment.”

“Where is its original filename?”

“The program recovered it from the cache after the input stopped.”

“Creation time?”

“It belongs at the end of the recording.”

“That is a position in your project, not a device timestamp.”

Evan opened the clip properties. The fields for source creation and recorder time were blank. Only the import time remained.

3:46 p.m.

Four minutes after the test began.

Abby stepped beside Diane. “Was the camera audio still running?”

“Yes,” Evan said.

“Then compare it.”

They turned to the camera.

Its red light had gone out.

Abby pressed the display button. The screen showed a battery symbol and the final saved clip. She opened the file information.

The recording ended at 3:45:12.

“Battery cut,” she said.

“When?” Diane asked.

Abby scrubbed through the final seconds. The image showed Ron raising his hand, the rush behind him, Diane crossing to the laptop, and Evan protesting the end of the test.

The file stopped before the disputed phrase.

No continuous camera record covered the moment when RECOVERED\_07 entered the project cache.

Ron set the tripod down carefully.

“Enough,” he said.

Evan stared at the blank metadata fields. The missing support weakened his certainty, but he continued searching for another explanation.

“The software recovered a buffer,” he said. “That still means the microphone captured it.”

“It means the software produced a clip from cached data,” Diane said. “We cannot establish when the sound entered the buffer, which input carried it, or whether your playback bled into the recording path.”

“It says behind you.”

“It resembles those words after you supplied them.”

“You heard it before I said them.”

“I heard noise.”

Ron faced the stairs. “Argue upstairs.”

Diane closed the laptop with both hands.

